\title{The disjunctive property}
\author{reidbarton}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-06-08}
\date{2026-06-09}
\date{2026-09-14}


\subtree[bak-E550]{
\taxon{Idea}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\contributor{reidbarton}
\date{2026-09-14}
\p{
Given a lattice #{\II}, \em{the disjunctive property} states that if
#{(i \lor j) = 1} for any #{i\, j : \II} then #{i = 1} or #{j = 1}. We show that if
\ol{
\li{
The interval is [directed](Synthetic.Directedness): any endomap on #{\II} is
monotone.}
\li{The booleans cover the crisp elements of #{\II}.}
\li{Types with an initial object separate.}
}
then #{\II} satisfies the disjunctive property.
}

\p{
The above listed conditions generalise the following respective axioms from
Triangulated Type Theory \citek{GWB24}:
\ol{
\li{The interval is [quasicoherent](Synthetic.Quasicoherence).}
\li{There are precisely two crisp elements of #{\II}.}
\li{Finite cubes separate.}
}
}
}

